    <statement><p>Calculate the gradient of <m>f(x,y)=\cos\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)</m></p>
<p><m>\nabla f(x,y)=</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left&lt;-8x\sin\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right),3\sin\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)\right&gt;</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>f(x,y)=\cos\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)</m> using the Chain Rule:
<me>\frac{\partial{f}}{\partial{x}}=-\sin\left(4x^{2}-3y\right) \frac{\partial}{\partial{x}}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)=-8x\sin\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)</me>
<me>\frac{\partial{f}}{\partial{y}}=-\sin\left(4x^{2}-3y\right) \frac{\partial}{\partial{y}}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)=3\sin\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)</me>
The gradient vector is thus
<me>\nabla f=\left\lt -8x\sin\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right),3\sin\mathopen{}\left(4x^{2}-3y\right)\right&gt;</me>





</solution>
